  "dom_id": "step-q89774",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/assets/245037d572c2bc9c81d3cdfb7a485b167c15223c68e3dbb8e40eaa2cc7650da9.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the hyperbola shown above given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm x.$",
  "worked_solution": "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\nx=b\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t,\\,y=a\\operatorname{sec}\u2061t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(0,\\pm a).$ From the graph, we have that $(0,-1)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=1.$\n\nRecall also that the asymptotes of the hyperbola occur at $y=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a}{b} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{b} & =1 \\\\ b & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sec}\u2061t.\n$$",
